{"records":[{"id":"3zygrwcj4t","title":"The contribution of native protein complexes to targeted protein degradation","description":"Targeted protein degradation (TPD) destroys proteins of interest (POIs) by hijacking the cellular proteolytic machinery. Most proteins in cells exist and function as part of multi-protein or macromolecular complexes, thereby allowing a single protein to control multiple biological processes. Therefore, when a small molecule degrader induces proximity between an E3 ligase and the POI, the macromolecular context of the POI potentially influences the degradation outcomes of the POI and of the complex components. Here, we explore degradation of the eight CK1α-SACK1 (formerly known as FAM83A-H) complexes initiated by molecular glue degraders primarily designed to target Ser/Thr kinase CK1α. We demonstrate that lenalidomide-derived degraders DEG-77 and SJ3149, which selectively target the CK1α isoform, co-degrade multiple SACK1 proteins. We show that the degradation of SACK1 proteins by DEG-77 and SJ3149 requires CK1α, the CUL4ACRBN E3 ligase complex and the proteasome. In cells derived from palmoplantar keratoderma patients harbouring the CK1α-binding deficient SACK1GR265P mutation, DEG-77 targets CK1α and mitotic SACK1D but not SACK1GR265P, highlighting the requirement for CK1α-SACK1 interaction to achieve co-degradation. Our study underscores the importance of POI context in TPD and reinforces the potential for selectively targeting specific protein complexes for degradation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-29T13:52:59.172Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3zygrwcj4t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3zygrwcj4t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lorraine","last_name":"Glennie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nicole","last_name":"Curnutt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gajanan","last_name":"Sathe","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Brune","last_name":"Le Chatelier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Freya ","last_name":"Goff","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jin-Feng","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tyrell ","last_name":"Cartwright","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Karen","last_name":"Dunbar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nicola","last_name":"Wood","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Macartney","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christina ","last_name":"Woo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gopal","last_name":"Sapkota","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology","Protein Degradation","Chemical Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"vfjjtwstw9","title":"Zalm et al. technical drawings","description":"The are the technical diagrams for the radiation setup as described in the STAR Protocols paper \"Local irradiation of patient-derived tumors in immunodeficient mice\" . ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-11-24T07:17:10.39Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/vfjjtwstw9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/vfjjtwstw9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maarten","last_name":"Bijlsma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Amber","last_name":"van der Zalm","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Arlene","last_name":"Oei","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0368jnd28","name":"Dutch Cancer Society","grant_id":"10992"}],"categories":["Radiation","Esophageal Cancer","Mouse Model","Xenograft"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fpvmmm33gd","title":"RNA-seq data for F1-F6-RiboTAC in MDA-MB-231 cells","description":"This is a supplementary dataset to a manuscript we are publishing.  This dataset includes the RNA-seq results quantifying the change in transcript abundances after treating with small molecule RNA degraders in MDA-MB-231 cells.  Please refer to our manuscript once published for the complete details.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-04-09T09:59:33.253Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fpvmmm33gd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fpvmmm33gd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yuquan","last_name":"Tong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matthew","last_name":"Disney","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pvv4t2b342","title":"Single-cell RNA sequencing of PDX tumors before and after drug treatment","description":"Subcutaneous PDX tumors (PDX) were established in female B-NDG mice using patient-derived HCC tissues.  Tumor samples were collected at day 16 for 10x single-cell RNA-seq","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-10T08:25:12.529Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pvv4t2b342","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pvv4t2b342"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xiaofeng","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Single-Cell Transcriptomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gcdd36j296","title":"A study of 'TRIP12 promotes small molecule–induced degradation of BRD4 through K29/K48 branched ubiquitin chains' by Kaiho-Soma et al.","description":"Raw data presented in 'TRIP12 promotes small molecule–induced degradation of BRD4 through K29/K48 branched ubiquitin chains' by Kaiho-Soma et al.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-22T06:08:41.836Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gcdd36j296","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gcdd36j296"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Fumiaki","last_name":"Ohtake","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2d8wzkcn3v","title":"CELL-CHEMICAL-BIOLOGY-D-17-00243 Shimada et al.","description":"This dataset contains i) metabolomic profiling performed by Metabolon, ii) FPKM-normalized RNA-seq data performed by the Stockwell lab and Columbia Genome Center, and iii) 83 compounds identified to induce similar mechanism of lethality in cancer cell lines, for the manuscript \"Copper-binding small molecule induces oxidative stress and cell cycle arrest in glioblastoma-patient-derived cells\" written by Shimada et al.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-07-25T23:28:55.694Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2d8wzkcn3v","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2d8wzkcn3v"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kenichi","last_name":"Shimada","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metabolomics","RNA Sequencing","Chemical Compound"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gjvtx65n8y","title":"Data for: Functional imaging and targeted drug delivery in mice and patient tumors with a cell nucleolus-localizing and tumor-targeting peptide","description":"Source data for \"Functional imaging and targeted drug delivery in mice and patient tumors with a cell nucleolus-localizing and tumor-targeting peptide\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-08-01T15:15:22.818Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gjvtx65n8y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gjvtx65n8y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Renwei jing","last_name":"Jing","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Drug Delivery"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k3ph59xrtz","title":"Global RNA-seq for a RNA targeted small molecule that triggers elimination of r(G4C2)exp in c9ALS/FTD ","description":"This is a supplemental dataset for a manuscript studying a a RNA targeted small molecule that triggers elimination of r(G4C2)exp in c9ALS/FTD.  The effect of the small molecule on the human transcriptome was analyzed by RNA-seq in both iPSCs from the patient and the healthy donor.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-06T17:22:20.836Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k3ph59xrtz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k3ph59xrtz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Bush","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuquan","last_name":"Tong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matthew","last_name":"Disney","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8jrgxtcd5z","title":"p75 Neurotrophin Receptor Exacerbates Neuronal Apoptosis After Intracerebral Hemorrhage by Promoting OTUB1 Ubiquitination and Degradation","description":"p75 Neurotrophin Receptor Exacerbates Neuronal Apoptosis After Intracerebral Hemorrhage by Promoting OTUB1 Ubiquitination and Degradation-raw data","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-21T00:38:13.877Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8jrgxtcd5z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8jrgxtcd5z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jiabing","last_name":"Shen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z46cyfd8tz","title":"Parkin_mediated_mitophagy_is_induced_by_the_probiotics_Saccharomyces_boulardii_and_Lactococcus_lactis","description":"Dysfunctional mitochondria are a hallmark feature of neurodegenerative disorders, such as Parkinson’s\ndisease, and upregulation of Parkin-mediated mitophagy pathways serve to remove unhealthy\nmitochondria from cells. Probiotics have been used to alleviate symptoms of Parkinson’s disease and\nthere is evidence to suggest that they modulate mitochondrial pathways. In this study, we conducted a\nscreen of 49 probiotics and tested various stages of mitophagy to determine whether any of the\nprobiotics can upregulate mitophagy in cells sustaining mitochondrial damage. We found that two\nprobiotics, Saccharomyces boulardii and Lactococcus lactis upregulate mitochondrial Parkin recruitment,\nphospho-ubiquitination, and mitofusin degradation. Further, these strains increase lysosomal\ndegradation of dysfunctional mitochondria in the brain of Drosophila that were subjected to the\nmitochondrial toxin paraquat, and also improve longevity and motor function in this Drosophila model of\nParkinson’s disease. These effects occur due to exogenous soluble factors that are released by probiotics\nunder laboratory conditions. We identified methyl-isoquinoline-6-carboxylate as one such molecule,\nwhich upregulates mitochondrial parkin recruitment, phospho-ubiquitination, mitofusin degradation, and\nlysosomal degradation, while restoring motor function in paraquat-treated flies. These data suggest a\nnovel mechanism that probiotics use to stimulate mitophagy through a Parkin-dependent pathway that\ncould potentially be utilized to treat neurological diseases at an early stage.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-09-29T09:55:33.564Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z46cyfd8tz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z46cyfd8tz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Peter","last_name":"Hawrysh","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Mitophagy"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":140162,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":137,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":140162,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":139395,"restricted":70,"embargoed":697},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2322},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1379},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":699},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":473},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de Sã